WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.075
A schedule can fit its dates and still ask one person to
be in two places at once.

00:00:06.195 --> 00:00:09.470
This lesson adds the people side of the plan.

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:18.465
We will assign work, read allocation, inspect capacity and
overload, and examine the relationship between effort and
elapsed working time.

00:00:18.585 --> 00:00:22.960
The example deliberately gives Alice two overlapping
assignments.

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:28.955
The goal is to recognize the problem and resolve the plan
rather than merely hide its warning.

00:00:32.467 --> 00:00:36.592
Select Design and examine its resource assignment section.

00:00:36.712 --> 00:00:39.787
Alice is assigned at one hundred percent.

00:00:39.907 --> 00:00:45.307
An allocation describes how much of that person is
assigned to this task while it runs.

00:00:45.427 --> 00:00:52.127
It does not automatically prove that the person is
available, and it does not change their overall capacity.

00:00:52.247 --> 00:00:57.122
Compare the assignment in the inspector with the authored
resource statement.

00:00:57.242 --> 00:01:04.592
Consistent names matter: different spellings can make one
real person appear as two resources in workload reporting.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:13.850
The duration entered for assigned work represents effort
in the scheduling calculation.

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:19.795
When additional allocation is available, the same effort
can occupy fewer working days.

00:01:19.915 --> 00:01:25.865
This is a planning model, not a guarantee that every task
can be accelerated by adding people.

00:01:25.985 --> 00:01:30.385
Review the effective start and end as well as the authored
effort.

00:01:30.505 --> 00:01:35.680
We will add another full allocation to Backend and compare
the calculated result.

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:40.000
Keep a note of the original duration so the effect is
clear.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:46.925
Inspect Backend before changing its assignments.

00:01:47.045 --> 00:01:51.820
Sam is allocated at fifty percent, with eight authored
days of work.

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:56.515
Add Alex at fifty percent and confirm both people in the
source.

00:01:56.635 --> 00:02:01.760
After the preview refreshes, read the working duration and
resulting dates.

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:07.905
Verify the actual schedule rather than assuming that every
additional person halves a task.

00:02:08.025 --> 00:02:11.075
Here the authored work remains eight days.

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:17.200
Choose Plan and then Workload.

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:23.170
The panel groups assignments by person and compares their
planned allocation with capacity.

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:32.290
Alice appears on both Design and Frontend during the same
period, so the plan asks for two hundred percent while her
capacity is one hundred percent.

00:02:32.410 --> 00:02:36.110
Read the peak figure and inspect the affected dates.

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:44.530
A red warning is a prompt to investigate the underlying
assignments, not proof that increasing the capacity number
is the right solution.

00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:51.575
Select Frontend in the workload panel to open its task
inspector and investigate the assignment directly.

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:59.725
The Summary control switches between daily and weekly
workload views.

00:02:59.845 --> 00:03:06.595
Daily detail helps you locate a specific collision, while
weekly grouping helps you understand a longer pattern.

00:03:06.715 --> 00:03:12.290
Inspect the tasks and overloaded dates instead of reading
only one peak value.

00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:19.210
You can use task navigation and resource filtering to
focus the canvas, then return to the complete plan.

00:03:19.330 --> 00:03:26.480
Remember that hiding another person's tasks from the view
does not remove those assignments from the underlying
schedule.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.900
Capacity describes how much availability the plan allows
for a resource.

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:40.245
Change Alice's capacity to two hundred percent and observe
the comparison.

00:03:40.365 --> 00:03:46.215
The calculation now treats those overlapping assignments
as within the declared capacity.

00:03:46.335 --> 00:03:53.235
This is appropriate only if Alice represents a team or
another resource that actually has that availability.

00:03:53.355 --> 00:03:59.155
For an individual person, a larger capacity number can
conceal an unrealistic plan.

00:03:59.275 --> 00:04:05.900
Record the assumption clearly, and restore a realistic
capacity before choosing how to resolve the work.

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:19.875
There are several honest ways to resolve a conflict: move
work, reduce the allocation and accept a longer elapsed
duration, or assign an appropriate available person.

00:04:19.995 --> 00:04:25.445
Here we reassign Frontend to Alex so Alice is no longer
scheduled on both tasks.

00:04:25.565 --> 00:04:29.940
Inspect the updated workload and check the other people
too.

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:35.110
Solving Alice's problem by overloading Alex would simply
move the conflict.

00:04:35.230 --> 00:04:39.780
Keep the schedule consequences visible while choosing the
resolution.

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:48.175
The Gantt preferences allow you to disable the over-
allocation warning banner.

00:04:48.295 --> 00:04:57.145
That can be useful when an assignee name represents an
entire team, but it does not change workload calculations
or repair a scheduling conflict.

00:04:57.265 --> 00:05:00.540
Open Settings and find the warning preference.

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:06.185
Compare this presentation choice with the resource
capacities stored with the document.

00:05:06.305 --> 00:05:12.505
Be explicit about which kind of setting you changed,
especially when sharing a plan with other people.

00:05:16.033 --> 00:05:19.858
The workload panel can lead directly to a task check-in.

00:05:19.978 --> 00:05:27.203
This keeps the follow-up connected to the actual
assignments rather than requiring you to reconstruct who
owns each task.

00:05:27.323 --> 00:05:31.523
Inspect the report scope and dates before copying any
message.

00:05:31.643 --> 00:05:37.143
The app prepares the content; it does not send a message
to your team automatically.

00:05:37.263 --> 00:05:42.188
The reporting lesson will explain the options and other
report types in detail.

00:05:45.700 --> 00:05:48.675
Pause and open the workload example.

00:05:48.795 --> 00:05:54.645
Identify the tasks that overlap for Alice and state her
peak allocation and capacity.

00:05:54.765 --> 00:05:59.740
Choose one realistic resolution and explain its scheduling
consequence.

00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:05.960
Then compare daily and weekly summaries and check that
your change has not overloaded someone else.

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:11.530
Finally, distinguish changing an assignment, changing
capacity and hiding a warning.

00:06:11.650 --> 00:06:18.875
You have succeeded when the plan is feasible under
assumptions you can explain, not simply when the screen
has no red banner.

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:28.175
Assignments, capacity and working calendars together
determine what the resource model says.

00:06:28.295 --> 00:06:33.595
Use the inspector for task details and the workload panel
for the wider picture.

00:06:33.715 --> 00:06:42.090
Treat effort and elapsed working time as different
quantities, and use warning preferences only for their
intended presentation purpose.

00:06:42.210 --> 00:06:51.410
In the next lesson we will compare forecast and scenario
alternatives, using the same habit of checking the
assumptions before applying a proposed change.
